Recompiler: movz and movn move only the low 64 bits of the register, like the reference.

What it fixes: SPECIAL_MOVZ / SPECIAL_MOVN were translated with SET_GPR_VEC(GPR_VEC), which copies all 128
bits of $rs into $rd. The reference emulator's interpreter (third_party/pcsx2/pcsx2/R5900OpcodeImpl.cpp,
Interpreter::OpcodeImpl::MOVZ / MOVN) copies only UD[0]; the upper 64 bits of $rd keep their value (the
condition tests UD[0] of $rt in both). It only shows when a 128-bit (multimedia) register holds data in its
upper half, i.e. code that mixes lq/MMI with movz/movn on the same register. Found by tests/conformance
(family "compare"): movz failed 13 of 169 cases and movn 156 of 169, all in the upper half of $rd.

How it was verified: the conformance tests of compare pass with a recompiler relinked with this change and
fail without it (the pytest command of tests/conformance, see journal 025). The game has movz / movn sites
(docs/knowledge/data/instruction_conformance.tsv); whether any of them reads the upper half afterwards is not
known. Apply it only together with a regeneration and a regression run.

Original work for this repository (no code from another project).

